WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.794 --> 00:00:10.965
Amia and her friend Zelba have been
inseparable for nearly a year.

00:00:11.299 --> 00:00:16.095
They share secrets, play games,
and rule over imaginary kingdoms.

00:00:16.596 --> 00:00:20.349
Amia loves to talk,
and Zelba is a great listener.

00:00:21.142 --> 00:00:26.022
Amia sometimes has bad ideas,
and Zelba goes along with them anyway.

00:00:26.564 --> 00:00:31.110
Amia is very real, and Zelba... is not.

00:00:31.611 --> 00:00:36.574
Some parents worry about their child
having playmates they can't see or hear,

00:00:36.574 --> 00:00:41.037
but imaginary companions are a normal part
of psychological development

00:00:41.037 --> 00:00:42.205
for many kids.

00:00:42.205 --> 00:00:47.251
In fact, they even provide a special kind
of support real friends can’t.

00:00:47.376 --> 00:00:48.586
But to understand this,

00:00:48.586 --> 00:00:52.048
we first need to explore
what leads certain kids

00:00:52.048 --> 00:00:54.509
to create these fictional figures.

00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:59.222
Most often, their inventors are simply
looking for someone to play with.

00:00:59.388 --> 00:01:04.227
Imaginary friends are typically created
by children roughly 3 to 4 years old

00:01:04.227 --> 00:01:06.646
who lack siblings of a similar age.

00:01:06.854 --> 00:01:09.774
These kids tend to be socially outgoing—

00:01:09.816 --> 00:01:12.860
they like company,
so they invent more of it.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.239
And while almost all kids
enjoy playing pretend,

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:21.035
children with invisible companions
are especially fond of make-believe.

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:24.831
One study even found these kids
were better storytellers,

00:01:24.831 --> 00:01:26.833
suggesting that imagining friends

00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:29.919
might help with linguistic
and cognitive development.

00:01:30.002 --> 00:01:33.005
Outside a love of fantasy and socializing,

00:01:33.005 --> 00:01:37.426
experts haven't found any meaningful
differences in personality, intelligence,

00:01:37.426 --> 00:01:41.681
or shyness between kids who do
and do not have imaginary friends.

00:01:41.848 --> 00:01:45.852
However, the invisible characters
themselves couldn't be more different.

00:01:46.352 --> 00:01:48.855
They can be animals, real or fictional.

00:01:48.896 --> 00:01:51.566
Humans of all ages, shapes, and sizes,

00:01:51.566 --> 00:01:53.985
with features straight out
of a fairy tale.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:58.698
Some come alone; others have a gaggle
of imaginary friends and family.

00:01:58.823 --> 00:02:02.827
They can even be what researchers
call personified objects—

00:02:03.035 --> 00:02:06.455
stuffed animals or toys
brought to life by the imagination.

00:02:06.622 --> 00:02:10.042
And these manifestations
vary from place to place.

00:02:10.084 --> 00:02:15.631
A 2004 study found roughly 67%
of US kids under 8

00:02:15.631 --> 00:02:17.550
had invisible companions,

00:02:17.842 --> 00:02:22.013
while in Japan personified objects
were much more common.

00:02:23.055 --> 00:02:26.142
These fictional figures
don't just look different,

00:02:26.142 --> 00:02:28.477
they also fill a variety of roles.

00:02:29.437 --> 00:02:32.106
Many are playmates and confidants,

00:02:32.106 --> 00:02:36.861
but others are scapegoats,
errand buddies, or babies in need of care.

00:02:36.944 --> 00:02:40.448
This wide range is why researchers
typically call them

00:02:40.448 --> 00:02:45.661
invisible or imaginary “companions,”
since they’re not always friends.

00:02:45.703 --> 00:02:48.331
In fact, researchers
have even reported children

00:02:48.331 --> 00:02:52.668
who fight with their imaginary companion
or are actively afraid of them.

00:02:52.793 --> 00:02:55.504
These situations can concern parents.

00:02:55.713 --> 00:02:58.090
But not only are they totally normal,

00:02:58.174 --> 00:03:01.886
they highlight the special power
of imaginary companions:

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:07.725
each of these characters is designed
to fulfill their creator’s needs or wants.

00:03:08.768 --> 00:03:11.896
By creating a scary invisible companion,

00:03:12.271 --> 00:03:16.275
a child might be experimenting
with feeling fear and how to conquer it.

00:03:16.817 --> 00:03:21.656
Negotiating imaginary disagreements
lets kids practice dealing with conflict

00:03:21.656 --> 00:03:23.866
without real-world consequences,

00:03:23.866 --> 00:03:28.287
and resolving these arguments can help
them explore empathy and caregiving.

00:03:28.412 --> 00:03:29.914
In these ways and more,

00:03:29.914 --> 00:03:35.294
invisible companions give developing
children the agency to explore emotions

00:03:35.294 --> 00:03:38.005
and social interactions on their terms.

00:03:38.089 --> 00:03:41.884
And since research consistently shows
children know these characters

00:03:41.884 --> 00:03:43.010
are imaginary,

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:47.807
the kids are always in control—
even when it doesn’t look like it.

00:03:47.974 --> 00:03:50.893
Some invisible companions
stick around through their creator’s

00:03:50.893 --> 00:03:52.395
teenage and adult years.

00:03:52.687 --> 00:03:57.566
But generally, as kids start making
friends in school around age 5 or 6,

00:03:57.858 --> 00:04:01.737
they talk about their imaginary
companions less and less often.

00:04:02.446 --> 00:04:05.825
That said, the time they spent
with these fictional figures

00:04:05.825 --> 00:04:07.785
has lifelong benefits.

00:04:08.077 --> 00:04:13.040
Research suggests pretend play
strengthens a child's theory of mind.

00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:16.961
This is our ability to understand,
imagine, and predict

00:04:16.961 --> 00:04:18.879
other people’s mental states—

00:04:19.338 --> 00:04:23.884
a skill that can help kids develop
empathy and build relationships.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:27.388
And while we might stop speaking
to our invisible companions,

00:04:27.388 --> 00:04:30.141
we never stop talking to ourselves.

00:04:30.474 --> 00:04:33.269
When you rehearse a difficult conversation
in the shower

00:04:33.311 --> 00:04:35.438
or talk through your problems to a pet,

00:04:35.521 --> 00:04:39.442
you’re using the same skills you learned
from your imaginary companions—

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:43.946
talking through your inner monologue
to problem solve and regulate your mood.

00:04:44.613 --> 00:04:47.908
So, the next time you see
a kid chattering to thin air,

00:04:47.992 --> 00:04:52.330
know that you're watching the beginning
of a lifelong conversation.
